1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:12,225
A whole-brain Drosophila connectome model predicts a duration- and sign-dependent potassium trap after anoxia, and a reset-based route to functional recovery.

2
00:00:13,467 --> 00:00:26,439
The model is a leaky integrate-and-fire network on the FAFB version 783 connectome: one hundred and thirty-nine thousand neurons, and three point seven million connections.

3
00:00:26,589 --> 00:00:41,539
A phenomenological energy and potassium layer spans seventy-nine neuropils, with diffusion, regenerative potassium release, depolarisation block, and an injury integral that leads to stochastic neuron death.

4
00:00:42,800 --> 00:00:49,713
Validation of the LIF layer against the reference whole-brain model (details in the paper).

5
00:00:50,933 --> 00:00:58,380
Intact model: per-neuron spike counts drive neurite glow (FAFB v783 skeletons, 5,307-neuron subset).

6
00:00:58,530 --> 00:01:09,795
LC4 100 Hz → DNp01 217/223 Hz (L/R) during the 300-ms stimulus.

7
00:01:09,945 --> 00:01:19,099
Glow persistence (trail) is a display choice; timing is the simulated 5-ms epochs, slowed ~27×.

8
00:01:19,249 --> 00:01:28,765
Sugar GRNs 150 Hz → MN9 132 Hz.

9
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:39,708
Anoxia (supply = 0) from t = 2 s; neurite hue tracks per-neuron ATP, haze tracks per-neuropil [K+]o.

10
00:01:39,858 --> 00:01:48,582
Spreading depolarisation: mean [K+]o > 15 mM 7.2 s after onset; max 44.7 mM.

11
00:01:48,732 --> 00:01:57,224
Burst ≤ 18.5 Hz/neuron, then depolarisation block; mean ATP 11% at 32 s.

12
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:08,828
Naive (abrupt) reoxygenation after 30 s, default sign rule [K02].

13
00:02:10,946 --> 00:02:20,569
696-neuron driver set leads (~296 vs 42 Hz mean); centred on antennal-lobe LNs — not established [K14].

14
00:02:20,719 --> 00:02:31,707
Self-sustained K+-coupled runaway, peak 304 Hz/neuron; ionic, survives removal of all synapses [K07].

15
00:02:31,857 --> 00:02:43,442
AL local neurons inhibitory: 30-s runaway 0/3 FAFB [K03]; after 180 s it persists (MCNS naive FII_norm −0.135) [K04].

16
00:02:44,667 --> 00:02:54,929
Quiet and active states coexist; K+ clamp at 5 mM collapses the runaway (2.4 Hz/neuron); hysteresis ≥ 13 mV [K07].

17
00:02:56,133 --> 00:03:01,724
20-s total activity block at reoxygenation (block_all).

18
00:03:03,938 --> 00:03:11,726
[K+]o < 6 mM by t = 36 s; ATP 94% at 40 s; released network stays at baseline.

19
00:03:13,276 --> 00:03:26,006
FII_norm 1.001 / 0.953 at 30 / 180 s anoxia; 2–5 s blocks fail; works when started 20 s late [K05].

20
00:03:33,587 --> 00:03:47,156
Probe after reset: MN9 132 Hz (intact 132 Hz). Releasing at K+/ATP normalisation (11 s) re-ignites [K06].

21
00:03:48,400 --> 00:03:58,023
180 s anoxia: runaway in 48/48 parameter sets (FAFB) and 5/5 MCNS runs; rescue 48/48 at 30 s, 34/48 at 180 s [K01, K05].

22
00:03:59,267 --> 00:04:11,556
Post-reset loss is neuron death; top-1% hub protection ~30× (FAFB) and 84× (MCNS) more efficient per neuron [K09].

23
00:04:12,800 --> 00:04:21,548
'Brain death' (silent, unresponsive) only with ≥ 97.9% of neurons dead (92 of 1,209 FAFB runs) [K08].

24
00:04:22,800 --> 00:04:47,057
Limitations. This is a simplified model, with constants not fitted to Drosophila. The thirty-second trap is sign-sensitive, and the driver core is likely a sign artefact. The cooling effect is set by the injury rule. And wiring predicts recovery after the reset, but not trapping. None of this is evidence about living tissue, clinical brain death, or human resuscitation.

25
00:04:48,267 --> 00:04:56,183
